// DESIGN MOCKUP -- not yet the real inventory.bcl case study (that
// migration is tracked separately, GitHub issue #66, blocked on this file
// stabilizing). try/catch itself is real now, on both targets (issue
// #60), but with a real gap on --target C: it only catches a raise from
// try_body's own top-level statements (error, or a failed sequential
// open ... for input) -- the same raise sites on error goto already
// supports, and restricted the same way (rejected inside a function/
// procedure body). A raise from inside a *called* procedure (checkPart(),
// editRecord(), ... -- what this actual case study needs) still isn't
// caught on --target C; that needs the struct-return propagation
// proposed further down, still unimplemented.
//
// This is the proposed C-port shape for tutorial/inventory.bcl. The
// try/catch syntax is deliberately structured: a failed menu action is
// abandoned and recovery continues after `end try`. It does not reproduce
// arbitrary BASIC RESUME NEXT instruction-pointer behavior inside a
// procedure.
//
// The BASIC backend transpiles this to ON ERROR GOTO plus a recovery
// label and RESUME to that label (implemented -- see codegen_basic.rs's
// own `try_catch`). The C backend reuses that same ON ERROR GOTO/bcc_err/
// bcc_erl machinery for its own restricted top-level-only support (see
// codegen_c.rs's own `Statement::TryCatch` arm in `emit_statement`) --
// the full status-propagation version for calls into procedures is the
// struct-return proposal below, not yet implemented.
//
// Proposed shape for that C-side propagation: every generated (and
// wrapper/stdlib) function and procedure returns a small struct bundling
// its real value alongside a status code --
//   typedef struct { int status; int    value; } bcc_result_int;
//   typedef struct { int status; long   value; } bcc_result_long;
//   typedef struct { int status; float  value; } bcc_result_single;
//   typedef struct { int status; double value; } bcc_result_double;
//   typedef struct { int status; }               bcc_result_void;
// (a `procedure`, and any function whose payload already goes out through
// the existing `bcc_out` string parameter, use bcc_result_void). Every call
// site's handling is decided purely by lexical context, no whole-program
// reachability analysis needed: inside a `try`, a nonzero status `goto`s
// its catch label; inside a function/procedure with no enclosing `try`, it
// `return`s the whole struct upward unchanged; at true top level with
// nothing further to propagate to (main, outside any `try`), it falls into
// the same fatal exit(1) path `emit_raise_block` already uses today for an
// uncaught error.
//
// The real cost of doing this uniformly: a call can no longer sit inline
// inside an arbitrary expression the way it does today (`x = someFunc(1) +
// 2`), because its status has to be checked the instant it returns, before
// anything downstream of it runs. Every call, everywhere in the program --
// not just ones inside a `try` -- needs hoisting to its own statement-level
// temp first:
//   bcc_result_int bcc_tmp2 = someFunc(1);
//   if (bcc_tmp2.status) return (bcc_result_double){ .status = bcc_tmp2.status };
//   x = bcc_tmp2.value + 2;
// That's an ANF-style flattening pass over every expression containing a
// call, program-wide -- not something scoped to try/catch alone.
//
// err% and erl% in `catch err%, erl%` are ordinary locals scoped to the
// catch block, not aliases for BASIC's ambient (readable-anywhere) ERR/ERL.
// They're assigned once, at the top of the generated catch block, from
// whatever the runtime captured when the error was raised, and go out of
// scope at `end try`. This means the C backend never needs to expose its
// internal error-status globals outside of that one assignment.
//
// Those internal globals (bcc_err/bcc_resume_id, already file-scope statics
// -- see ERROR_HANDLING_GLOBALS in codegen_c.rs) stay global rather than
// becoming locals threaded through each raise site: a try block can be
// entered/failed from several different raise sites via goto, and one of
// them writing straight into a per-catch local (rather than a global) would
// make that local's own declaration+initializer something a *different*
// raise site's goto could land past unset -- the same declaration/goto
// ordering hazard that ordinary user-declared locals avoid by being hoisted
// to the top of the enclosing C function with a `= 0` initializer (see
// `emit_function_def` in codegen_c.rs), before any goto in that function --
// including today's `on error goto`/`resume` -- can run. err%/erl% get the
// analogous fix: the values live in globals, always-initialized and
// writable from any raise site with no declaration for a goto to jump past,
// and the catch (and any future `finally`) block copies them into its own
// locals as its first statement, regardless of which raise site got there.
//
// bcc_resume_id (see ERROR_HANDLING_GLOBALS/emit_raise_block in
// codegen_c.rs) is a per-raise-site integer ID, not a real BASIC line
// number -- this backend never tracks those. It does two jobs today: it's
// what ERL reads, and it's the switch key plain top-level RESUME/RESUME
// NEXT dispatch on to `goto` straight back to that site's own
// bcc_raise_retry_<id>/bcc_raise_after_<id> label, literally resuming
// execution at the failed statement. That second job only works because
// on error goto raise sites are restricted to top-level code today -- the
// raise site and its handler are the same C function, so a goto back is
// legal. A try's raise can happen many call frames deep (checkPart() ->
// some helper -> some deeper helper), and by the time its status has
// bubbled up through bcc_result_void returns to the frame holding `catch`,
// every one of those inner C stack frames is already gone -- there's no
// goto that could land back inside checkPart() mid-statement even in
// principle, since C has no cross-function jump at all. So erl% inside
// `catch` can only ever be informational, exactly like reading ERL is --
// resume/resume next genuinely cannot be offered inside a catch block.
// That's what this file's own opening paragraph already commits to (no
// arbitrary RESUME NEXT instruction-pointer behavior); bcc_resume_id's
// call-frame-based dispatch is the concrete reason why.
program inventoryTryCatch

require com.bascal.stdlib.error

// The existing Part record and inv file declarations remain unchanged.
// The existing checkPart(), editRecord(), listAll(), addStock(),
// subtractStock(), and reorderReport() procedures are unchanged too.

procedure reportInventoryError(err%, erl%)
    locate 25, 1
    print "There has been an error on line" + str$(erl%) + ": " + error$(err%)
    waitAnyKey()
end procedure

do
    showMainMenu()
    kp$ = readKey$()

    try
        select case kp$
            case "1", "c", "C"
                checkPart()
            case "2", "e", "E"
                editRecord()
            case "3", "l", "L"
                listAll()
            case "4", "a", "A"
                addStock()
            case "5", "s", "S"
                subtractStock()
            case "6", "r", "R"
                reorderReport()
            case "7", "q", "Q"
                quitFlag% = 1
            case "8", "x", "X"
                inv.close()
                end
        end select
    catch err%, erl%
        // Recovery abandons the complete menu action and resumes here,
        // after the try block, with no dead procedure frame to revisit.
        reportInventoryError(err%, erl%)
    end try
loop until quitFlag% = 1

inv.close()
end
